  "base_commit": "f51b24096795a15f3d0aae6c860398e8470bfe0f",
  "pr_number": 2543,
  "issue_number": 2535,
  "issue_title": "Bug: Re-inviting a user whose prior (unaccepted) invitation has expired raises an unhandled 500",
  "issue_body": "## Summary\n\nWhen an admin invites an email address that already has an **expired, never-accepted** invitation in the same family, `InvitationsController#create` raises `ActiveRecord::RecordNotUnique` (Postgres `unique_violation`). It is not rescued, so the user gets the generic Rails 500 page (\"We're sorry, but something went wrong\"), and the admin can never re-invite that person through the UI until the stale row is removed from the database by hand.\n\nThe root cause is a semantic mismatch between what the application treats as a \"pending\" invitation (unaccepted **and** not expired) and what the database's partial unique index treats as pending (unaccepted only). The model-level duplicate guard therefore does not fire, `save` proceeds, and the `INSERT` collides with the index.\n\nThis is independent of mailer configuration: on a self-hosted instance no invitation email is sent at all (see `#create`), so the failure is purely the unhandled DB constraint violation.\n\n## Affected code (current `main`)\n\n- `app/models/invitation.rb` \u2014 `pending` scope, `pending?`, `no_duplicate_pending_invitation_in_family`, `no_other_pending_invitation`, `set_expiration`\n- `app/controllers/invitations_controller.rb` \u2014 `#create`\n- Partial unique index `index_invitations_on_email_and_family_id_pending` (definition in `db/schema.rb`; created by migration `db/migrate/20260314120000_remove_unique_email_family_index_from_invitations.rb`)\n\n## Steps to reproduce\n\n1. As a family admin, invite a new email address. An `Invitation` row is created with `accepted_at = NULL` and `expires_at = 3.days.from_now`.\n2. Do not accept it. Let `expires_at` pass (or set it to the past for a fast repro).\n3. As the same admin, invite the same email address again.\n4. `POST /invitations` returns HTTP 500. The log shows:\n\n```\nActiveRecord::RecordNotUnique (PG::UniqueViolation: ERROR: duplicate key value\nviolates unique constraint \"index_invitations_on_email_and_family_id_pending\")\n```\n\nMinimal console repro:\n\n```ruby\ninv = family.invitations.create!(email: \"repro@example.com\", role: \"member\", inviter: admin)\ninv.update_column(:expires_at, 1.day.ago)   # simulate an expired, unaccepted invite\n\nfamily.invitations.create!(email: \"repro@example.com\", role: \"member\", inviter: admin)\n# => ActiveRecord::RecordNotUnique: PG::UniqueViolation ...\n#    \"index_invitations_on_email_and_family_id_pending\"\n```\n\n## Root cause\n\nTwo definitions of \"pending\" disagree.\n\nApplication (`app/models/invitation.rb`):\n\n```ruby\nscope :pending, -> { where(accepted_at: nil).where(\"expires_at > ?\", Time.current) }\n\ndef pending?\n  accepted_at.nil? && expires_at > Time.current\nend\n```\n\nDatabase partial unique index (`db/schema.rb`):\n\n```ruby\nt.index [\"email\", \"family_id\"],\n  name: \"index_invitations_on_email_and_family_id_pending\",\n  unique: true, where: \"(accepted_at IS NULL)\"\n```\n\nThe duplicate guard `no_duplicate_pending_invitation_in_family` queries the `pending` scope. Because an expired invitation is excluded by the `expires_at > now` clause, the validation does not see the stale row and `save` proceeds. The `INSERT` then hits the partial unique index, whose predicate is only `accepted_at IS NULL`, so the expired-but-unaccepted row still occupies the unique slot and Postgres raises `unique_violation`.\n\n`ActiveRecord#save` returns `false` only for validation failures; a database constraint violation raises. `#create` calls `save` inside an `if` without rescuing `ActiveRecord::RecordNotUnique`, so the exception surfaces as a 500.\n\n## The index cannot be made time-aware\n\nA tempting fix is to add `AND expires_at > now()` to the index predicate so it matches the model's notion of pending. This is not possible in PostgreSQL: partial-index predicates (and index expressions) must be `IMMUTABLE`, and `now()` / `CURRENT_TIMESTAMP` are `STABLE`, not `IMMUTABLE`. The fix must therefore live in the application layer (or change the data-model semantics) \u2014 it cannot be solved by making the index time-aware.\n\n## Suggested fixes (for the maintainer to choose / combine)\n\n- **A \u2014 Align the validation with the index (graceful failure).** Have `no_duplicate_pending_invitation_in_family` test all unaccepted invitations (mirror the index predicate `accepted_at IS NULL`, ignoring expiry), so a collision is reported as a normal validation error instead of raising. On its own this would also block legitimate re-invites of expired invitations, so it is best combined with C.\n- **B \u2014 Rescue the violation in the controller (safety net).** Catch `ActiveRecord::RecordNotUnique` around the save and convert it to a flash alert (optionally locating the existing record). Cheap, defensive, and valuable regardless of which other option is chosen \u2014 nothing in `#create` should be able to 500 on a duplicate.\n- **C \u2014 Treat an expired, unaccepted invite as replaceable (best UX).** On create, if an unaccepted invitation already exists for `(normalized_email, family_id)`, refresh it instead of inserting a new row (regenerate the token, reset `expires_at`, update the role/inviter) \u2014 or destroy-then-create inside a transaction. This makes \"re-invite\" behave the way users expect and eliminates the duplicate at the source.\n\nRecommended combination: **C** for the behavior, plus **B** as a safety net; optionally **A** so the app-layer and DB-layer notions of uniqueness are explicitly consistent.\n\nRelated: `no_other_pending_invitation` (the cross-family guard) also relies on the `pending` scope and may exhibit analogous edge behavior, though it is not backed by a unique index. Worth reviewing in the same change.\n\n## Regression test (illustrative)\n\n```ruby\ntest \"re-inviting an expired, unaccepted invitation does not raise a 500\" do\n  admin = users(:family_admin) # adapt fixture name\n  sign_in admin\n\n  inv = admin.family.invitations.create!(email: \"repro@example.com\", role: \"member\", inviter: admin)\n  inv.update_column(:expires_at, 1.day.ago)\n\n  assert_nothing_raised do\n    post invitations_url, params: { invitation: { email: \"repro@example.com\", role: \"member\" } }\n  end\n  assert_response :redirect\n  # If Option C is implemented, also assert the invitation was refreshed and is now pending:\n  # assert admin.family.invitations.find_by(email: \"repro@example.com\").pending?\nend\n```\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- Re-inviting an email whose prior invitation is unaccepted and expired never raises `ActiveRecord::RecordNotUnique` and never returns a 500.\n- The admin gets a clear outcome: a fresh/refreshed pending invitation, or a friendly validation message.\n- A regression test covers the expired-unaccepted re-invite path.\n\n---\n\nI'm happy to open a PR for this \u2014 I'd suggest **B** (the controller safety net) plus **C** (refresh the expired invitation), with the regression test above. Let me know which combination you'd prefer and I'll put it together.\n",
  "merged_at": "2026-07-08T06:59:54.000Z",
